<DocsLayout
  title="Code review"
  eyebrow="Capabilities"
  description="Run a read-only reviewer over a diff, get prioritized structured findings, and publish them as pull request review comments."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/codex-parity-tools/", title: "Codex-parity tools" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/tool-search/", title: "Provider tool search" }}
>
  <p>
    <code>/review</code> runs a reviewer over a diff on a <strong>detached, read-only
    thread</strong> and returns prioritized findings anchored to file and line ranges.
    Findings land in a TUI panel where you keep or drop each one, and the kept set can be
    submitted to a review platform through a pluggable publisher. The same flow is
    available over the app server as <code>review/start</code>, so an external client can
    put Roder into review mode without a terminal.
  </p>

  <h2>Run a review</h2>
  <pre><code>/review                      # staged, unstaged, and untracked changes
/review --base main          # everything since the branch diverged from main
/review --commit a1b2c3d     # one commit
/review --uncommitted        # explicit form of the default
/review check error handling in the new parser   # free-form scope</code></pre>

  <p>
    Anything that is not a recognised flag becomes a free-form scope, so the last form
    needs no quoting. <code>--publish &lt;id&gt;</code> does <em>not</em> publish on its
    own — it preselects which publisher the panel's <kbd>p</kbd> key will use, so
    submitting a review stays an explicit action.
  </p>

  <h2>How the review turn runs</h2>
  <p>
    Roder resolves the target into a natural-language prompt (using the merge base for
    <code>--base</code>), creates a child thread of the requesting thread, and starts a
    single turn on it with the reviewer rubric installed as the system prompt. That thread
    carries a read-only tool allowlist: the reviewer can read files, search, and run
    <code>git diff</code>, but nothing that writes files, edits code, or applies patches is
    advertised or dispatchable.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the turn finishes, the reviewer's final message is parsed into structured findings
    and echoed back into the <em>requesting</em> thread as a synthetic
    <code>&lt;user_action&gt;</code> record plus a rendered summary. The parent agent can
    therefore act on the findings in its next turn — "fix the P1s" works — without the
    review turn ever having had permission to edit anything itself.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The review runs in a separate thread.</strong> Findings are advisory
    records in your thread's history. Nothing is applied, committed, or pushed as a side
    effect of running <code>/review</code>.
  </div>

  <h2>Findings</h2>
  <p>
    Roder has no provider-independent structured-output channel, so the rubric asks for a
    single JSON object and the parser recovers it with a four-tier ladder: the whole
    message, the contents of a fenced block, the first balanced <code>&#123;…&#125;</code>
    substring, and finally the raw text carried as the explanation. Tiers 1–3 also
    normalize snake_case keys and numeric priorities, so a model that drifts from the
    schema still produces usable findings instead of an empty review.
  </p>

  <pre><code>&#123;
  "findings": [
    &#123;
      "title": "[P1] Guard the divisor before dividing",
      "body": "…markdown explaining why this is a problem…",
      "confidenceScore": 0.74,
      "priority": "p1",
      "codeLocation": &#123;
        "absoluteFilePath": "/repo/src/calc.py",
        "lineRange": &#123; "start": 16, "end": 20 &#125;
      &#125;
    &#125;
  ],
  "overallCorrectness": "patch is correct",
  "overallExplanation": "…1-3 sentences…",
  "overallConfidenceScore": 0.85
&#125;</code></pre>

  <p>
    Priorities run <code>p0</code> (blocking) through <code>p3</code> (nice to have) and
    default to <code>p2</code>. The rubric deliberately suppresses findings the author
    would not act on: pre-existing bugs, pure style, and anything demanding more rigor than
    the surrounding code already has. A review that finds nothing worth reporting is a
    normal, healthy result.
  </p>

  <h2>The review panel</h2>
  <p>
    Findings open in a panel over the transcript. Every finding starts kept; drop the ones
    you disagree with before publishing.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><kbd>↑</kbd>/<kbd>↓</kbd> move, <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd> jump.</li>
    <li><kbd>Space</kbd> keep or drop the selected finding.</li>
    <li><kbd>a</kbd> keep all, <kbd>d</kbd> drop all.</li>
    <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> open the full body, <kbd>Esc</kbd> back or close.</li>
    <li><kbd>p</kbd> publish the kept findings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Publishers</h2>
  <p>
    Publishing goes through a <code>ReviewPublisher</code> extension service. Core resolves
    a publisher by id from the extension registry and calls it — it knows nothing about
    GitHub specifically.
  </p>

  <pre><code>#[async_trait]
pub trait ReviewPublisher: Send + Sync + 'static &#123;
    fn descriptor(&amp;self) -&gt; ReviewPublisherDescriptor;
    async fn is_available(&amp;self, workspace_root: &amp;Path) -&gt; bool &#123; true &#125;
    async fn publish(&amp;self, request: ReviewPublishRequest)
        -&gt; Result&lt;ReviewPublishResult, ReviewPublishError&gt;;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>ReviewDestination</code> is free-form — <code>&#123; publisherId, target, options
    &#125;</code> — so a publisher interprets <code>target</code> however it needs: a pull
    request reference, an issue id, a chat channel. Each
    <code>[review.publishers.&lt;id&gt;]</code> config block is stored opaquely and parsed
    by the publisher's own crate, so adding a platform never edits a core config type, and
    a block for a publisher you have not installed is ignored rather than rejected.
  </p>

  <p>Publisher selection resolves in order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><code>publisherId</code> on the <code>review/publish</code> request, or <code>--publish &lt;id&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><code>[review].default_publisher</code>.</li>
    <li>The only installed publisher, when exactly one is installed.</li>
    <li>Otherwise an error naming the installed candidates.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>GitHub publisher</h2>
  <p>
    <code>roder-ext-github-review</code> submits findings as a pull request review. It
    prefers the <code>gh</code> CLI when it is installed and falls back to the REST API
    with a token, selectable with <code>mode</code>. With no explicit destination it
    resolves the pull request for the current branch.
  </p>

  <pre><code># ~/.roder/config.toml
[review]
default_publisher = "github"   # omit, or "none", to always ask
model = "opus"                 # optional reviewer-specific model

[review.publishers.github]
mode = "auto"                  # auto | cli | http
event = "COMMENT"              # COMMENT | REQUEST_CHANGES | APPROVE
min_priority = "p2"            # drop anything less severe
token_env = "GITHUB_TOKEN"     # http mode only</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>event</code> defaults to <code>COMMENT</code>, which never changes a pull
    request's state. Approving or requesting changes on your behalf is opt-in.
  </p>

  <h3>Anchoring comments to the diff</h3>
  <p>
    GitHub rejects an <em>entire</em> review with a 422 if any single comment names a path
    that is not in the pull request or a line outside its diff hunks — so a partially
    unmappable review would otherwise post nothing at all. Roder therefore prefilters:
    every finding is checked against the hunk spans parsed from the pull request's file
    patches, and anything that cannot be anchored is moved into the review summary body and
    reported in <code>skipped</code> with a reason, instead of poisoning the request.
  </p>
  <p>
    Commentable lines are the full post-change span of each hunk, context lines included —
    not just added lines. Multi-line findings emit <code>start_line</code> only when the
    range genuinely spans more than one line, and paths are relativized to the git repo
    root rather than the workspace root, which differ under worktree forks.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Preview before you post.</strong> <code>dryRun: true</code> on
    <code>review/publish</code> returns the exact JSON payload GitHub would receive,
    including the prefilter's decisions, without making any network call.
  </div>

  <h2>Over the app server</h2>
  <p>
    Three methods and four notifications expose the same flow to any client.
    <code>review/start</code> takes a <code>threadId</code>, a <code>target</code>, and a
    <code>delivery</code> of <code>inline</code> (await the findings) or
    <code>detached</code> (return immediately and receive them as events).
  </p>

  <pre><code>&#123;
  "method": "review/start",
  "params": &#123;
    "threadId": "thread-123",
    "target": &#123; "kind": "baseBranch", "branch": "main" &#125;,
    "delivery": "detached"
  &#125;
&#125;

// -> &#123; "turnId": "…", "reviewId": "…", "reviewThreadId": "…" &#125;
// -> review/started notification
// -> review/completed notification, carrying the parsed findings</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>review/publish</code> submits a stored review by <code>reviewId</code>, optionally
    narrowed to <code>findingIndexes</code>, and <code>review/publishers/list</code>
    enumerates installed publishers and their capabilities. Notifications are
    <code>review/started</code>, <code>review/completed</code>,
    <code>review/failed</code>, and <code>review/published</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding another platform</h2>
  <p>
    Supporting a different review platform is three mechanical steps and touches no shared
    crate:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Add a <code>crates/roder-ext-&lt;platform&gt;-review</code> crate implementing
      <code>ReviewPublisher</code> and parsing its own
      <code>[review.publishers.&lt;id&gt;]</code> block.
    </li>
    <li>Hand that block to the crate from the extension host's review module.</li>
    <li>Install it in the default registry.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Nothing in the API types, core, protocol, app server, or TUI changes.
    <code>review/publishers/list</code> picks the new publisher up automatically and
    <code>--publish &lt;id&gt;</code> can select it.
  </p>
